  "findings": [
    "Both services and protected input remain READY. One unchanged authenticated connection per side; no recorded fault, reconnect, recovery-failure or worker-poison event in this build. B reports 22 successful input-readiness receipts.",
    "Twelve exact exchange-tag paired profiles all completed preparation successfully. Detailed profiling is capped at the first 12 attempts and 120 seconds; these are not maxima for all 22 entries.",
    "No admission-drain-started/completed event occurred. This run shows no recurrence, but did not exercise the new late-inspection recovery branch and cannot prove that the patch caused the absence of faults.",
    "Ten profiled entries reused a candidate and two found an expired candidate. Attempts 6 and 11 spend 399.232ms and 345.353ms connecting, including 177.844ms and 148.790ms native-pair readiness and 193.657ms and 175.388ms service bootstrap validation. These local nested spans cannot be added.",
    "The median receiver preparation falls markedly while its maximum worsens. The baseline had four expired candidates out of eleven, so preparation-cohort changes explain part of the median difference; this is not a controlled performance benchmark.",
    "A capture-activation maximum is lower, but its median is slightly higher. B profiled input dispatch rises from 3.674ms median / 7.134ms maximum to 9.680ms / 21.863ms. Do not describe all latency as improved.",
    "Next latency target remains the actual expiry-to-replacement/native-pair path. Increasing a timeout or treating source grant wait as pure network delay would not remove the measured B-local preparation work.",
    "Runtime summaries after the first detailed profile also contain B control dispatch 412.643ms and A control dispatch 159.194ms. These are control-processing spans, not native SendInput durations, and cannot be assigned to a particular capped handoff profile. Timer lateness peaks at 86.681ms on B and 78.091ms on A in those summaries."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Host wall clocks differ; no subtraction of cross-device timestamps is used to estimate one-way network delay.",
